  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft continues to be headlined by a expansive \nhigh pressure system over the southeastern portion of the CONUS. The \nlow pressure system over the Pacific Northwest continues to hold \nsteady near the Oregon/California border. Nearer to the surface and \nthe CWA, a weak surface low pressure system resides in northeastern \nColorado. \n\nToday will continue the pattern of hot and mostly dry. Both highs \nare forecast in the mid-90s. The surface low is expected to induce \nvery weak thunderstorms tonight, but all CAMs have either no \nconvection or weak convection northwest of the forecast area. Friday \nwill be similar from a precipitation perspective with \nweak/marginal storms northwest of the CWA. There is a little \nmore uncertainty with some CAMs having some storms scrapping the\nnorthwestern counties, but ensemble chances remain <15%. \n\nSaturday's highs are expected to be similar, but holds a little more \nuncertainty based on a timing of a cold front. Ahead of the cold \nfront in the afternoon/evening, storms are expected to develop in \nthe eastern counties. These storms are expected to remain marginal \nif not sub-severe. More time will be needed to observed the pattern \nand the timing in the front for a better idea for the conduciveness \nof the environment. More widespread showers are expected behind\nthe front well into the overnight. QPF values have widespread \n0.1-0.15\" with the obvious caveat that some areas will see more \nand some will see less. The driest area that will likely receive\nno rain is in far SW Kansas with the wettest areas in the \nnorthern counties. \n\nSunday will lead off next week considerably cooler. Highs are \nforecast in the upper 70s/lower 80s through Thursday. Every single \none of these days has at least 30% chance or greater for \nprecipitation via ensembles. Future forecasts will provide more \nclarity and specifics about the potential storms once temporal \nuncertainty is reduced. Relative humidity minimums will remain well \nabove 25% quelling most of the fire weather risk with only an \nelevated risk Thursday and Friday with strong south winds.",
